== Stage 13 ==
;17 July 2026 – [[Dole, Jura|Dole]] to [[Belfort]], {{convert|205.8|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:2026-07-17 17-31-27 TDF-Belfort.jpg|thumb|[[Mauro Schmid]] and [[Harold Tejada]] approaching the finish line in [[Belfort]]]]
Heading north-east towards the [[Vosges]] mountains, the thirteenth stage from [[Dole, Jura|Dole]] to [[Belfort]] was the longest of the race at {{convert|205.8|km|abbr=on}}. Two climbs featured in the last {{convert|50|km|abbr=on}} – the third-category climb of Col de Croix, followed by the first-category climb of [[Col du Ballon d'Alsace|Ballon d'Alsace]] (8.9km at an average gradient of 6.9%). Riders then descended to the finish in Belfort.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 13 - Dole > Belfort - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-13 |access-date=2026-07-05 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=126, 128}}</ref>

Stage 13 was won by [[Mauro Schmid]] ({{UCI team code|JAY men|2026|nolink=}}) from a breakaway, with [[Tom Pidcock]] ({{UCI team code|PQT|2026|nolink=}}) leaping up to 4th overall owing to his positioning in the breakaway.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=2026-07-17 |title=Tom Pidcock surges up standings while Mauro Schmid wins Tour de France stage 13 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/jul/17/tom-pidcock-surges-up-standings-while-mauro-schmid-wins-tour-de-france-stage-13 |access-date=2026-07-24 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-07-17 |title=Mauro Schmid outduels Harold Tejada on stage 13 at Tour de France |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-mauro-schmid-outduels-harold-tejada-in-two-rider-matchup-at-the-line-on-stage-13/ |access-date=2026-07-24 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref> 
