== Stage 14 ==
;18 July 2026 – [[Mulhouse]] to [[Le Markstein]] [[Fellering]], {{convert|155.3|km|abbr=on}}
[[File:2026-07-18 13-43-33 TDF-Bollwiller.jpg|thumb|Peloton on stage 14]]
Remaining in the Vosges mountains, the fourteenth stage from [[Mulhouse]] to [[Le Markstein]] [[Fellering]] had four categorised climbs over {{convert|155.3|km|abbr=on}}. The first-category climb of [[Grand Ballon]] (21.5&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 4.8%) was followed by the second-category Col du Page, another ascent of the first-category climb Ballon d'Alsace, and the first-category Col du Haag (11.2&nbsp;km at an average gradient of 7.3%) – at the summit of Col du Haag, the finish at Le Markstein was {{convert|5|km|abbr=on}} away along a plateau.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stage 14 - Mulhouse > Le Markstein Fellering - Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.letour.fr/en/stage-14 |access-date=2026-07-05 |website=www.letour.fr |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |title=2026 Tour de France Roadbook |publisher=Amaury Sport Organisation |pages=134, 136, 139-140}}</ref>

Stage 14 remained in the Vosges, with Pogačar attacking on the Col du Haag from the group of {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders. As Pogačar built an advantage on the plateau to the finish, Vingegaard and Seixas worked together to minimise the gap – eventually finishing around 40 seconds behind Pogačar, who now had a {{abbr|GC|general classification}} lead of around 4 and a half minutes. Vingegaard remained in second place overall, with Evenepoel in third. The other {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders – Seixas, Ayuso, Lipowitz and Del Toro – were all within 30 seconds of each other.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Owen |date=2026-07-18 |title=Tour de France: Tadej Pogačar’s late attack on Col du Haag nabs solo stage 14 win as Isaac del Toro outsprints Paul Seixas and Jonas Vingegaard to take second |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-tadej-pogacars-late-attack-on-col-du-haag-nabs-solo-stage-14-win-as-isaac-del-toro-outsprints-paul-seixas-and-jonas-vingegaard-to-take-second/ |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref>

